01.a|ACDFJ|Build a property-to-observer matrix for physical extent, logical field boundary, initialized value, stale lifetime and retained-resource budget. Give one original negative control each and predict which observer can miss it.|Addressability implies initialized and semantically valid data.|A legal-address wrong-field write needs an application invariant; initialized-value and lifetime checks have distinct scopes; each capability claim has a negative control.|Move the same mechanism into a custom pool whose objects share one allocated region.|A tool's allocation boundary can be wider than a logical object's boundary.|Custom allocation may require tool annotations or independent object-state checks. Detector visibility must be demonstrated, not inferred from the tool name.
01.b|BCDFJ|Run one original negative/repaired pair in separate supervised processes with matching inputs and exact build identities. Verify setup and an entry marker before interpreting report, signal, timeout or silence.|A setup crash or unavailable runtime is evidence about the intended defect.|Only trials reaching the target count as observations; negative silence is inconclusive and repaired silence also requires semantic checks.|Remove instrumentation from one dependency and repeat the capability control.|Record setup, target entry and target outcome as separate stages.|A supervisor timeout bounds the experiment but does not diagnose the cause. Instrumentation coverage and generated-code survival must be checked.
01.c|ACFIJ|Review proposed golden tests expecting a particular crash address, signal and uninitialized value from three UB examples. Replace them with invariant-based analysis and scoped actual diagnostic observations.|Undefined behavior has a portable reproducible symptom or clean output proves legality.|No prescribed UB result remains; the corrected implementation has defined semantic expectations and the negative run is observational evidence only.|Change optimization and explain why differing symptoms neither repair nor further define the original program.|Locate the language violation before discussing the machine symptom.|Compiler optimization can exploit violated assumptions. A finite consistent symptom does not turn undefined behavior into an implementation-defined contract.
02.a|BCFGJ|Implement the parent's fill, bounded scan and byte-to-string publication with capacity, initialized length, signed index and overlap policy explicit. Exhaust every index/count combination in a small declared domain, including one-past zero-length operations.|Capacity equals valid payload length or zero-length permits forming any pointer.|Negative and beyond-one-past indices reject before conversion/pointer formation; one-past with zero access follows the parent contract; NUL space is reserved.|Introduce a subview with independent logical length and shared backing capacity.|Validate scalar bounds before deriving addresses.|The caller still owes valid live storage. Scalar checks do not prove arbitrary pointer extent, lifetime or synchronization.
02.b|ACDFJ|Trace lower-bound, exact upper-bound, missing-NUL and embedded-NUL cases before running the checked operations. Identify the first forbidden pointer formation or access, not only the final error return.|An out-of-range pointer may be formed safely as long as it is not dereferenced.|The analysis rejects invalid formation where the C rule requires it; a bounded scan never reads the first absent byte; embedded NUL follows the publication contract.|Use a length-delimited binary payload that legitimately contains zero bytes.|String and byte-span contracts accept different languages.|A binary span is not a C string. Reusing a string helper can silently truncate data or impose an unwanted terminator rule.
02.c|BCDFGJ|Snapshot every destination byte and published length before each rejected operation. Add a legal backing-array write to index6 with logical_limit4 and demonstrate a semantic monitor that catches it while an extent-only check does not.|A quiet memory detector proves the logical write was correct.|Rejected checked operations preserve all observable state; the logical-cell mutant is rejected independently of addressability.|Allow overlapping source/destination under a newly versioned move contract and derive staging direction.|A successful address check is only one precondition.|Overlap changes the implementation proof and can expose partial mutation. A new contract needs an independent oracle rather than inheriting disjoint-copy evidence.
03.a|ABCFJ|Derive checked arithmetic for count*stride+header+terminator under a declared limit, testing each intermediate before evaluation. Compare the parent9360/9361/9363 cases with actual SIZE_MAX-derived boundaries.|Checking the final wrapped result repairs an earlier overflowing intermediate.|The parent distinctions remain success65532, addition failure and multiplication failure; output publishes only on complete success.|Add alignment rounding and derive the extra checked addition before masking/division.|Prove each subtraction/division precondition.|Alignment padding can overflow even when payload size is valid. A power-of-two mask formula also needs a stated positive alignment constraint.
03.b|ACDFIJ|Classify negative signed count conversion, unsigned multiplication wrap, signed multiplication overflow and narrowing to a smaller interface width. Provide mathematically distinct counterexamples without executing UB as a result oracle.|Defined unsigned wrap is a sufficient allocation-size contract or all overflows are UB.|Language classification and application rejection are separate; narrowing is checked before information is lost; tool coverage is stated precisely.|Compare a C23 checked-arithmetic interface only after inspecting its exact edition contract, retaining the C17 implementation.|Write the mathematical value and destination representable range first.|Edition comparison cannot be inferred from a compiler accepting an extension. Diagnostics and runtime checks add evidence beyond the language's baseline guarantees.
03.c|BCFGJ|Connect the size helper to a counted allocation seam and inject refusal at every accepted size. Verify zero allocator calls after size rejection, unchanged output/owner and correct cleanup after allocation refusal.|A failed size computation may still allocate or discard the old owner.|Each rejected arithmetic path has zero allocation calls; accepted-but-refused allocation preserves ownership under the parent contract.|Make allocation succeed but a subsequent initializer fail, and stage publication until cleanup completes.|Arithmetic admission and resource acquisition are different failure stages.|A single error code can hide different obligations. The acquisition ledger must distinguish resources never acquired from resources requiring rollback.
04.a|ABCFHJ|Compute a conditional call-path budget using actual .su qualifiers and explicit recursion, callback and interrupt nesting limits. Include an unknown indirect callee and separate MSP/PSP where applicable.|Adding known frame sizes closes unknown paths or one stack formula applies to every exception configuration.|The parent basic/extended sums remain conditional; unknown paths return no certified whole-path maximum; alignment and helpers are accounted for.|Permit a logging callback during fault handling and rederive the feasible nesting graph.|Only simultaneously live frames contribute to a path.|Reentrant diagnostics can add the very frame omitted from a failure budget. A feasible-path argument is necessary; summing arbitrary maxima is neither exact nor always conservative.
04.b|ACDHIJ|Compare changed-pattern scan, recorded minimum SP and actual frame reservation in a bounded test. Include untouched local space and bytes restored to the fill pattern; retain native exhaustion as a separate child-only observation.|Pattern high water equals maximum reserved stack or an exhaustion depth is portable.|The parent32changed versus48reserved discrepancy is explained; sampled values are lower evidence under stated assumptions, not universal bounds.|Enable a different optimization or FP-context configuration and recollect rather than reuse the number.|A reserved slot need not be written.|Instrumentation and compilation alter frames. Native termination demonstrates only the selected process/limits and must be distinguished from setup failure.
04.c|BCDFHJ|Repair a bounded recursion/large-frame case by converting recursion to an explicit caller-owned work stack with capacity and overflow status. Compare semantic outputs, stack use and failure preservation with local-buffer corruption as a different defect.|Increasing stack size repairs all stack corruption or iterative means unbounded work is safe.|The explicit stack has a checked bound and defined refusal; local extent corruption requires its own repair; no universal native exhaustion signal is assumed.|Use breadth-first work storage and analyze the changed peak occupancy.|Control-stack depth and algorithmic frontier size are different resources.|An iterative conversion trades one bounded resource for another. The representation and refusal contract must be assessed alongside correctness.
05.a|ACDFJ|Classify four supplied failures by first invariant: fixed section beyond region, zero range touching retained bytes, custom heap crossing its reservation and wrong legal-cell write. Require distinct evidence for each.|Runtime corruption means bss grew or link success proves runtime placement safety.|Only the first is fixed-region link overflow; startup, allocator and logical-write defects require their own transition evidence.|Replace the contiguous embedded region with hosted virtual memory and reject the transferred collision model.|State whether the violated boundary is link-time, startup or runtime.|The hosted allocator and process stack need not share the synthetic contiguous interval. Target layout assumptions cannot be generalized from a diagram.
05.b|ABCFJ|Implement a checked offset-interval layout certificate for data,bss,retained,heap,guard,stack with alignment. Recompute the parent560fixed bytes and heap gaps48/16; add near-limit alignment overflow and zero-size sections.|Alignment rounding and load/run addresses can be ignored in region arithmetic.|All endpoints are checked before addition; retained placement and guard constraints are separate; invalid intervals reject unchanged.|Give data a Flash load address and RAM run address and account for both capacities.|LMA and VMA consume different declared regions.|A copy image occupies load storage even though execution uses the run region. Link-map evidence and startup copy correctness are distinct obligations.
05.c|BCDFGJ|Repair a wrong zero-range ending at13 in the16-byte startup model so retained[12,16) remains unchanged. Test overlapping copy/zero ranges and refusal before the first write; separately retain an actual deliberately oversized experimental link failure.|Clamping after startup writes restores retained contents or a host buffer model proves pre-main board execution.|The invalid range is rejected without mutation; the corrected image matches explicit bytes and link/runtime evidence remains separately labeled.|Introduce a reset-retained checksum field and specify which resets preserve it.|Validate the complete range plan before applying writes.|Reset categories and retained-memory behavior are board contracts. A host array experiment cannot establish power-cycle persistence.
06.a|BCFGJ|Instrument every acquisition, transfer, borrow and release for the three-resource component; inject failure before and after each acquisition and before publication. Add cleanup failure while preserving the first cause.|A final initialized flag records all partial ownership obligations.|Every acquired resource is released once in dependency order or explicitly transferred; unacquired resources are never released; failed construction publishes nothing.|Transfer one child resource to another owner before parent failure.|Track ownership per resource identity, not per function return.|Rollback cannot reclaim a resource whose ownership was transferred successfully. The transfer boundary needs its own commit semantics.
06.b|ACDFHJ|Extend the finite ownership graph with an unreachable cycle, a root through an interior token and a reachable cache exceeding its budget. Compare semantic categories with separately observed native leak reports.|Reachable means acceptable retention or every RSS increase is a lost allocation.|Reachability and retention budgets are separate; cycle and conservative-root assumptions are explicit; no graph label is promised as a native detector string.|Add weak edges and recompute strong reachability.|Mark edge ownership and root kind before traversing.|A reference-counted cycle and a tracing collector have different reclamation behavior. The graph model must identify which mechanism is being assessed.
06.c|BCFGHJ|Repair cleanup and cache-retention policy, then run bounded repeated construct/use/destroy cycles with failure at every stage. Record live, retained and peak bytes plus release counts, not only exit leaks.|Process exit cleanup or a final zero count proves every operation maintained the budget.|Every cycle returns to its declared baseline; transient peaks and retained budget are checked at each boundary; first cause survives cleanup failure.|Let destruction be deferred until readers leave an epoch and update the retained-byte bound.|Delayed reclamation changes when zero live ownership becomes reusable storage.|Exactly-once release and bounded retention are related but different. Reclamation delays must be included in the resource model.
07.a|ACDFGJ|Implement the nonzero resize wrapper using a temporary result and deterministic refusal seam. On success initialize new tail bytes and rebuild all published borrows from the returned owner; use integer histories to model same-address reuse.|A same-address success preserves old aliases or assigning realloc directly to the owner preserves failure state.|Refusal retains old owner/content; success invalidates prior aliases regardless of numeric address and publishes only initialized payload.|Replace resize with allocate-copy-commit-free and compare failure guarantees.|Save required content and metadata before the lifetime transition.|Do not inspect or compare the old C pointer after successful realloc. Integer address tokens in a model do not authorize evaluating an indeterminate pointer.
07.b|ACDFJ|Diagnose double, interior, foreign and stale-generation releases using the supplied integer model, then one original isolated native negative control where supported. Keep repaired code free of invalid pointer evaluation.|A null assignment to one owner repairs every alias or raw equality validates release authority.|The model rejects each distinct authority violation; native symptoms remain observations rather than fixed outcomes.|Retire a generation at its maximum instead of wrapping and test capacity exhaustion.|Identity and release authority are more than an address.|Generation handles work only when all accesses use the protocol and storage remains live while validated. They do not retroactively make arbitrary stale pointers safe.
07.c|BCDFGJ|Test forced refusal, relocation and modeled address reuse across repeated resize/drop operations with two borrowers. Verify exactly-once release and require borrowers to reacquire after success.|Testing only moving allocation covers alias invalidation or free(NULL) repairs ownership.|Both modeled success cases expire old borrows; refusal preserves them; every owned generation has one release obligation.|Allow a borrower to outlive the resize call and choose pinning, copy or rejection with an explicit contract.|Choose the lifetime policy before changing the allocator.|Each policy changes resource costs and permitted operations. ISO C supplies no automatic borrow checker to enforce the chosen discipline.
08.a|ACDFJ|Create a field-state table for length,mode,payload pointer and owned-resource tag. Distinguish acquired storage, typed initialization, semantic validity and publication; reject a partially initialized active record.|malloc or zero-byte fill establishes every typed semantic state.|Publication requires all designated fields and invariants; all-zero object bytes are not assumed universally valid pointer/float representations.|Use a tagged union whose inactive member need not be initialized and specify legal reads.|Initialization obligations follow the active representation.|A tagged representation needs a valid tag-to-payload relation. Initializing every byte is neither necessary nor sufficient for every abstraction invariant.
08.b|ADFIJ|Trace a missing field write through a copied record to a later branch, then compare actual supported Memcheck observation with an address-only control. Record the first missing initialization separately from the report site.|A silent copy licenses evaluation of an indeterminate C value or the report site is the origin.|The source-level obligation is identified before the consuming branch; detector propagation behavior remains a tool model, not a language permission.|Use an uninstrumented library boundary and state what initialization evidence is lost.|Follow semantic field writes, not only byte accessibility.|Origin tracking can be approximate and instrumentation incomplete. An observed report supports its exercised scope; absence does not prove initialization everywhere.
08.c|BCDFGJ|Repair initialization with typed fields and staged publication, then vary initialized fill patterns and fail each acquisition. Compare records field-wise and serialize only explicit initialized fields.|Whole-struct memcmp implements semantic equality or padding is a wire format.|Equivalent semantic fields compare equal regardless of supplied nonsemantic bytes; failures publish no active object; serialization is deterministic by contract.|Add a versioned optional field and specify absent/default semantics.|Padding and inactive representation are not application state.|A portable wire format needs explicit sizes, byte order and version rules. Native layout and representation bytes cannot be imported silently.
09.a|ACFGJ|Draw separate intervals for request buffer, callback context, callback borrow and scratch through submit, timeout, cancel request, completion, callback return and reclaim. Add completion before submit returns.|Stopping the wait or receiving completion closes every access interval.|Reclaim waits for every possible accessor, including caller/callback control flow; cancellation intent alone leaves the borrow active.|Permit the callback to submit a successor sharing the context.|List accessors before choosing a release event.|A state machine with only pending/done may omit callback execution. The lifetime contract must cover the integration boundary, not just the device result.
09.b|BCDFGJ|Reject duplicate/stale completions and reentrant scratch reuse in the bounded generation protocol. Enumerate terminal-event orderings and test generation exhaustion without wrap; preserve state on denied reclaim.|Generation checks alone prove quiescence or reentrant callbacks can share one scratch buffer.|Exactly one terminal result ends the borrow; stale events cannot mutate the new generation; scratch conflict follows explicit exclusion/disjointness.|Allow nested callbacks with separate scratch leases and derive a finite nesting bound.|Logical identity checks and physical access cessation are separate proofs.|A late producer may still hold a raw address even when its event is rejected. Safe reclamation requires the producer's quiescence protocol as well.
09.c|ACDFGIJ|Compare the repaired hosted history with a target-evidence checklist for reachability, publication ordering, interrupts, cache maintenance where applicable and device stop acknowledgment. Use only a dedicated board/emulator route for future experiments.|TSan, a CPU mutex or debugger halt models independent DMA behavior.|Host evidence closes only the host protocol; target-specific requirements remain explicit, and no hardware activity is claimed without execution.|Move F439 SRAM buffers to an M7 cached region and derive the changed obligations from exact manuals.|Start with which agent can access which memory region.|Cache visibility, DMA reachability and ownership are different mechanisms. A generic event model cannot supply register-level cancellation or coherency proof.
10.a|ADFIJ|For two histories with the same final crash, construct competing extent, initialization, lifetime and capacity explanations and choose a discriminating observation before repair. Minimize the first violating prefix rather than the final symptom.|The crash site is the cause or one familiar symptom identifies the mechanism.|Each hypothesis predicts a distinct earlier state; the supported cause points to the earliest violated invariant and preserves rejected alternatives.|Use the same mechanism with a different delayed symptom.|Find the earliest state where the explanations disagree.|Changing layout can suppress a symptom without restoring the contract. The causal argument must survive that changed observation.
10.b|ADFIJ|Select a valid-object watch, allocation-generation history, linker map or bounded fault record according to the suspected defect. Include an unavailable region and an invalid fault-address flag; refuse invented values.|Every tool can observe every lifetime phase or a plausible fault address is valid.|The chosen evidence has explicit lifetime, timing, frame and availability limits; unknown state remains unknown.|Move the object during the replay and rebind the observation safely.|A tool is useful only while its observation preconditions hold.|Observation can perturb scheduling and layout. A stopped PC or watch event may be later than the causative transition.
10.c|BCDFGIJ|Freeze an invariant repair and compare it with canary/guard mitigation on the same original target. After seven days, solve an unseen capacity, signed-width, failure-prefix or completion-order variant before revealing the oracle.|A detected overwrite or changed allocation layout prevents the defect or a memorized fixture proves transfer.|The repair prevents the invalid transition; mitigation effects and residual limits are separate; unseen assessment remains unclaimed until administered.|Compose the repaired component with an asynchronous caller that changes ownership timing.|Ask which invariant the mitigation actually enforces.|Detection, containment and prevention are different properties. Integration can invalidate a previously adequate lifetime argument and requires new evidence.
